# Building the PostgreSQL Server Installer - macOS

This document covers everything needed to build the macOS PostgreSQL server
installer from this repository, from a clean build machine to a finished
package. See the [root README](../README.md) for what the installer bundles
and the overall repository layout.

## Build platform

The build platform for macOS is macOS 15 (Sequoia), used to produce the
universal (arm64 + x86_64) macOS packages - unlike the other supported
platform (Windows), macOS binaries can only be built on macOS itself (there
is no cross-compilation path; the CI job runs on a real macOS runner).

## Setting up a build machine

Install the Xcode Command Line Tools:

```
xcode-select --install
```

This provides the compiler toolchain and the macOS SDK the build links
against.

Install [Homebrew](https://brew.sh/), then install the utilities actually
needed to build the installer:

```
brew install cmake bison flex
```

`bison` and `flex` are required to build PostgreSQL's own grammar (the
versions Apple ships under `/usr/bin` are too old); `cmake` is needed for
the handful of dependencies below that use a CMake-based build instead of
autotools. (See "Set up DocBook" further down for the one other Homebrew
package needed.)

The old MacPorts-based setup, and its `ossp-uuid` dependency, are no
longer used - PostgreSQL is now built with `--with-uuid=e2fs` against the
e2fsprogs library, which is one of the third-party dependencies built
below.

Alternatively, a new build VM can be cloned from an existing one on the
same machine rather than set up from scratch:
- Shutdown the VM.
- Right click the VM, "Show in Finder", then right click the bundle to
  copy it under another name.
- Double-click the copy to power it on and choose "I copied it" when
  Fusion asks.
- Change the hostname/computer name:
  ```
  sudo scutil --set ComputerName "newname"
  sudo scutil --set LocalHostName "newname"
  sudo scutil --set HostName "newname"
  ```
- System Preferences -> Users & Groups and change the full name to match.
- Restart the VM.

## Building the third-party dependencies

Dependency libraries must be built with a little more control to ensure
they use the correct SDK and are built as universal (arm64 + x86_64)
binaries, usable on macOS 12 (Monterey) and above.

The exact list of third-party libraries PostgreSQL links against on macOS,
and the exact version of each, is pinned in [`packages-osx.txt`](packages-osx.txt).
At the time of writing that's: openssl, zlib, curl, zstd, lz4, libxml2,
libxslt, libiconv, icu, gettext, e2fsprogs, krb5, libedit.

To build one of these yourself:

1. Download the library's source tarball, matching the version pinned in
   `packages-osx.txt`, and unpack it.

2. Configure it to build as a universal binary against a fixed SDK. For a
   typical autotools-based library:

   ```
   SDK=$(xcrun --sdk macosx --show-sdk-path)
   CFLAGS="-arch arm64 -arch x86_64 -isysroot $SDK -mmacosx-version-min=12" \
   LDFLAGS="-arch arm64 -arch x86_64 -isysroot $SDK -mmacosx-version-min=12" \
   ./configure --prefix=/path/to/deps
   ```

   A few of these (e.g. zstd) use CMake instead - pass the equivalent flags
   as `-DCMAKE_OSX_ARCHITECTURES="arm64;x86_64" -DCMAKE_OSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=12
   -DCMAKE_OSX_SYSROOT="$SDK"`. OpenSSL uses its own `Configure` script and
   is typically built once per architecture and combined into a universal
   binary with `lipo`, rather than a single configure line.

3. Build and install it into a single common prefix (the `--prefix` above)
   that all the other dependencies, and PostgreSQL itself, will be built
   against:

   ```
   make -j"$(sysctl -n hw.ncpu)"
   make install
   ```

Repeat this for every library listed in `packages-osx.txt`, installing each
into that same prefix. Make sure any additional libraries link against
these freshly-built libraries, and not the older, system copies - for
libxslt, this means configuring with `--with-libxml-prefix`.

## Language interpreters

PostgreSQL is configured with `--with-python`, `--with-perl` and
`--with-tcl`, so three more things need to be in place first (see
[`version.txt`](version.txt) for the exact versions currently pinned, and
`scripts/osx/compile.sh` for how each is passed in):

- **Python** - install the official universal2 installer from python.org
  for the version pinned by the `python=` entry in `version.txt`, e.g.
  `https://www.python.org/ftp/python/<version>/python-<version>-macos11.pkg`.
  This installs a proper `Python.framework` under `/Library/Frameworks` -
  a Homebrew Python install will not work here, as plpython3 needs the
  framework layout. Only the build itself needs this exact pinned version:
  `compile.sh` rewires the resulting `plpython3.dylib` to link against
  `Python.framework/Versions/Current` rather than a fixed version, so any
  python.org install >=3.9 already on the end user's Mac satisfies it at
  runtime.

- **Perl and Tcl** - either build your own universal (arm64 + x86_64) Perl
  and Tcl matching the `perl=`/`tcl=` entries in `version.txt` the same way
  as the other dependencies above, or install the EDB Language Pack (which
  bundles both as universal builds already). Either way, point
  `compile.sh` at the resulting `PERL_DIR` (containing `bin/perl`) and
  `TCL_DIR` (containing `lib/tclConfig.sh`).

## Set up DocBook

```
brew install docbook docbook-xsl
```

Homebrew's `docbook-xsl` formula sets up a working XML catalog for you (at
`/opt/homebrew/etc/xml/catalog` on Apple Silicon,
`/usr/local/etc/xml/catalog` on Intel - see the `XML_CATALOG_FILES` line in
`compile.sh`). There's no need to manually download DocBook 4.2, patch its
catalog, or hand-write a catalog file. DocBook is used to build the
PostgreSQL documentation bundled with the installer.

## Build PostgreSQL

Once every dependency is installed into the common prefix, build
PostgreSQL itself against it:

```
server/scripts/osx/compile.sh <source-dir> <dep-prefix> <python-framework> <perl-dir> <tcl-dir>
```

`compile.sh` runs PostgreSQL's `./configure` with the flags this installer
needs (`--with-python`, `--with-perl`, `--with-tcl`, `--with-uuid=e2fs`,
DocBook catalog wiring, etc.), then `make` and `make install`. See the
script itself for the exact flag list.

After the build, `rewrite-dylib-refs.sh` rewrites the built binaries' dylib
load paths to be relocatable, so the installer doesn't embed the build
machine's paths:

```
server/scripts/osx/rewrite-dylib-refs.sh <staging-dir> <dep-lib-dir>
```

Then `prepare-staging-directory.sh` assembles everything (PostgreSQL
binaries, bundled extensions, licenses, docs) into the layout the macOS
installer actually packages up, and `create-debug-symbols.sh` extracts
`.dSYM` debug symbols from the staged Mach-O binaries before they're
stripped.

Finally, `installer.xml.in` (pre-processed into `installer.xml` with
version numbers etc. substituted in) is what BitRock InstallBuilder
actually builds into the `.dmg`/`.pkg`.

## Script reference (`scripts/osx/`)

Build-time scripts:

| Script | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `compile.sh` | Configures, builds and installs PostgreSQL itself against the dependency prefix. |
| `rewrite-dylib-refs.sh` | Rewrites built binaries' dylib load paths to be relocatable. |
| `prepare-staging-directory.sh` | Assembles the built files into the installer's staging layout. |
| `create-debug-symbols.sh` | Runs `dsymutil`/`strip` over the staged binaries to produce `.dSYM` debug symbol bundles. |
| `install-pgadmin.sh` | Downloads the latest community pgAdmin 4 build (matching the host architecture) and installs it to `/Applications` - the PostgreSQL 19+ path, now that pgAdmin is no longer bundled into the installer (see the root README). |
| `getlocales.sh` / `getlocales/getlocales.c` | Enumerates the locales available on the build/target system, for the installer's locale selection dropdown. |

Installer-time scripts (run on the end user's machine, not during the build):

| Script | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `preinstall.sh` | Pre-install checks (e.g. sufficient shared memory for PostgreSQL). |
| `prerun_checks.sh` | Checks the system temp directory is writable and scripts can execute from it. |
| `createuser.sh` | Creates the OS service user PostgreSQL runs as. |
| `initcluster.sh` | Runs `initdb` to create the data directory/cluster. |
| `loadmodules.sh` | Loads the PL language modules (via `scripts/common/loadplLanguages.sh`) into the new cluster. |
| `runpsql.sh` | Launches `psql` connected to the local server (used by the Terminal shortcut). |
| `createshortcuts.sh`, `createshortcuts_server.sh`, `createshortcuts_clt.sh`, `createshortcuts_sb.sh`, `createshortcuts_pgadmin.sh` | Create the Dock/Finder shortcuts for the server, command-line tools, StackBuilder and pgAdmin components respectively. |
| `doc-*.applescript.in` | Small AppleScript stubs (pre-processed with the install path substituted in) backing the "Documentation"/"Release Notes" Dock shortcuts - open the relevant bundled doc file in a browser. |
| `pgadmin.applescript.in` / `stackbuilder.applescript.in` / `psql.applescript.in` / `reload.applescript.in` | AppleScript stubs launching pgAdmin, StackBuilder, psql and a server reload respectively from their Dock shortcuts. |

Shared (`scripts/common/`):

| Script | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `loadplLanguages.sh` / `plLanguages.config` | Cross-platform: drives which PL language interpreters (Python/Perl/Tcl) get loaded into a newly-initialized cluster. |
